The fuzzer takes mruby source code as input. So the PoC is a Ruby program that triggers the heap-buffer-overflow in `memsearch_swar`.

The vulnerability is in `memsearch_swar` which is called via `mrb_memsearch` -> `mrb_str_index` -> `str_convert_range` -> `mrb_str_aref`. 

Let me understand the bug. Looking at `memsearch_swar`:
- `xs` is the pattern (length `m`)
- `ys` is the target string (length `n`)
- `s0 = ys`, `s1 = ys+m-1`
- `lim = n - m - sizeof(bitint)`
- The loop reads `sizeof(bitint)` bytes at `s0+i` and `s1+i`.

The issue: when `n - m < sizeof(bitint)`, `lim` becomes negative, so the loop doesn't execute. Then it falls to the second part:
```c
if (i+m < n) {
    const char *p = s0;
    const char *e = ys + n;
    for (;p<e;) {
      size_t len = e-p;
      p = (const char*)memchr(p, *xs, len);
      if (p == NULL || len < m) break;
      if (memcmp(p+1, xs+1, m-1) == 0) return (mrb_int)(p - ys);
      p++;
    }
}
```

Wait, let me think about the actual bug. The description says "during substring search the code can invoke memcmp past the end of the target buffer".

Let me look at the loop more carefully:
```c
for (i=0; i < lim; i+=sizeof(bitint)) {
    bitint t0, t1;
    memcpy(&t0, s0+i, sizeof(bitint));
    memcpy(&t1, s1+i, sizeof(bitint));
    ...
    for (size_t j = 0; zeros; j++) {
      if (zeros & MASK4) {
        const mrb_int idx = i + j;
        const char* p = s0 + idx + 1;
        if (memcmp(p, xs + 1, m - 2) == 0) {
          return idx;
        }
      }
      ...
    }
}
```

The `memcmp(p, xs + 1, m - 2)` reads `m-2` bytes starting at `s0 + idx + 1`. The valid range is `s0[0..n-1]`. So `p + (m-2)` must be `<= s0 + n`, i.e., `idx + 1 + m - 2 <= n`, i.e., `idx + m - 1 <= n`, i.e., `idx <= n - m + 1`. Hmm, actually `idx + m - 1 < n` means `idx < n - m + 1`.

Wait, the issue is the SWAR loop reads `sizeof(bitint)` bytes at a time. When `lim = n - m - sizeof(bitint)`, the loop runs while `i < lim`. The last iteration has `i` close to `lim`. At `i = lim - sizeof(bitint)` roughly... Actually `i` increments by `sizeof(bitint)`, so the last `i` value is the largest multiple of `sizeof(bitint)` less than `lim`.

Hmm, let me think about the actual reported bug. The key insight from the description: "during substring search the code can invoke memcmp past the end of the target buffer".

Let me look at the second loop again:
```c
if (i+m < n) {
    const char *p = s0;
    const char *e = ys + n;
    for (;p<e;) {
      size_t len = e-p;
      p = (const char*)memchr(p, *xs, len);
      if (p == NULL || len < m) break;
      if (memcmp(p+1, xs+1, m-1) == 0) return (mrb_int)(p - ys);
      p++;
    }
}
```

Here `len = e - p` is computed BEFORE `memchr` updates `p`. So after `p = memchr(...)`, `p` may have advanced, but `len` is stale (the old value). Then `if (p == NULL || len < m) break;` uses the stale `len`. So if `len < m` was false (because len was computed before p advanced), but actually after p advanced, the remaining bytes `e - p` could be less than `m`. Then `memcmp(p+1, xs+1, m-1)` reads `m-1` bytes from `p+1`, which could go past `e` (past the end of the buffer).

Wait, let me re-examine. `len = e - p` (old p). Then `p = memchr(p, *xs, len)`. memchr returns a pointer within `[old_p, old_p+len)` or NULL. So new `p >= old_p`. The remaining bytes from new p is `e - new_p <= e - old_p = len`. So `len` (stale) is an OVERESTIMATE of the remaining bytes. The check `len < m` uses the overestimate, so it might not break when it should. Then `memcmp(p+1, xs+1, m-1)` reads `m-1` bytes from `p+1`. The valid remaining from `p+1` is `e - p - 1`. We need `m-1 <= e - p - 1`, i.e., `m <= e - p`. But the check was `len < m` where `len = e - old_p >= e - new_p`. So if `e - new_p < m <= e - old_p`, the check passes (doesn't break) but `memcmp` reads out of bounds.

So this is the bug! The `len` is computed before `memchr` advances `p`, making it stale.

